#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105539 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3105e8eb-010f-542b-9e40-12fda6ea00e7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Matrix of a Stretch Transformation Given a Vector and its Image"}] :question/id #uuid "ffbe74ea-33e6-5998-8692-ed5d7bd0c30d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5450" :question/problem "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/0adb71786ea5de6203b155882b20000488d5467cf6dfcd34e6fa4e06ec091e46.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/94fb0fd9708c343c89fd98ff5b87c901c9e795026cc1dbd35d29cff92d6fe59e.png) what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-component of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ is $4$ times the $x$-component of $\\mathbf{v},$ while\n\n- the $y$-components of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ are the same.\n\nSo, we conclude that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch of scale factor $4$ in the $x$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/779a10caf7c07cbe4b5891240892db5433adc0f9f8895192a3a8bed5f9301126.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105540 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be42e4b3-4b15-589c-a7e1-23cc07083aff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Given the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "83ef303a-c25c-5cd5-8ab7-2c14cb05d433" :question/math-academy-id "e-5454" :question/problem "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ of $\\,\\mathbb{R}^2.$ If $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{i})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{j})=\\mathbf{j}$ then, what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/db15a65fd944c24ea2545baea08e6a0789b749295f623d8d4fdc2f07a37d4b73.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{i})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{j})=\\mathbf{j},$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/1e5310f50dab35bc25447af9b37d972e11eda0afee98ac99b87a819ba62f56a1.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a vertical shear with a shear factor of $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105541 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c36ce626-1562-51f8-b3fd-d7dc7ce8659c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Represented by a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "4d800cd6-01bd-561a-97cb-1bd672c019c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-5455" :question/problem "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/e335abcb649dae3ed9ade01017a23998614c3d0ff5a0898eb9b4d657f68e8b62.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ under the action of  $\\mathbf T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ T(j) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=2i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a *horizontal* shear with a shear factor of $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105542 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69bac3fa-d409-5c16-87b2-383423e8d754" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square Under a Shear or Stretch Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "043f45e2-97eb-540d-af0a-b04edb9ce60a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5456" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/611f958a7bd7475e37f743ef86affde7754195fe2c9ad414e628c17a09d47e38.png)\n\nLet ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/6231eb09a75376d694a6f47c47cf5a26aab4a32c28b2799f7e1f0825a7017f88.png) be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}.$ What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "Looking at the matrix $T,$ we can see that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a vertical shear of shear factor $-1.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/2d3d15c4d6b6f9e08c6fbcc35236b50dcd6cf4d09e9204e0aa255706461c3dd8.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the parallelogram shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c36ce626-1562-51f8-b3fd-d7dc7ce8659c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Represented by a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0528d85-1da8-5941-ab83-62315c90e1b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $4$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342567} {:answer/id #uuid "f588da42-80a1-53e1-a3e5-14ea56778b30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342568} {:answer/id #uuid "cd297346-42d7-5445-aa42-ca2c59f6a4fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $4$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342569} {:answer/id #uuid "a4219134-2b5c-5605-8706-c1a8391dd9cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342570} {:answer/id #uuid "97bedf08-9097-56ac-a45c-bb023dd83dea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f588da42-80a1-53e1-a3e5-14ea56778b30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342568} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e47b53-efed-5b84-8853-fe64728359c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57070669-d0cd-5bfc-9a7b-0eed859c86a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-12042" :question/problem "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of  $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ T(j) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=4i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a *horizontal* shear with a shear factor of $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c36ce626-1562-51f8-b3fd-d7dc7ce8659c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Represented by a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81b8ead8-163c-55e6-afa2-95af3681a7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102008} {:answer/id #uuid "9095931b-ff08-5a54-a2fb-955f264244c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $10$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102009} {:answer/id #uuid "955f0cc5-43f6-5a0e-af94-704b0c7a58c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102010} {:answer/id #uuid "b0555da6-a04e-5144-979c-b0a96959fdd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $10$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102011} {:answer/id #uuid "273e7475-d054-5f26-b9f1-7bb8aa9231b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81b8ead8-163c-55e6-afa2-95af3681a7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102008} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e5573e5-02b4-5a74-83f5-8f8e0b6b8adb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ec67f6b-b339-5863-9fd2-be6322d61819" :question/math-academy-id "q-12044" :question/problem "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of  $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ T(j) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=10i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a *horizontal* shear with a shear factor of $10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be42e4b3-4b15-589c-a7e1-23cc07083aff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Given the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad32dc1c-d6e5-519e-ad3a-64466060a392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch with a scale factor $2$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342574} {:answer/id #uuid "91c8635f-aef6-5dfe-8684-c185c31accf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342575} {:answer/id #uuid "fa242814-002b-5051-ab32-2b4072df1c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342576} {:answer/id #uuid "6891c862-6f77-5b0a-845a-e7db65e7cb7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch with a scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342577} {:answer/id #uuid "1332c767-1678-5804-b680-ed62af259dab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch with a scale factor $2$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad32dc1c-d6e5-519e-ad3a-64466060a392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch with a scale factor $2$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342574} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f268e18-580d-5fcb-8b81-6e9d1c818b8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0231dda-eca9-5f8d-b2ac-c6295636c34b" :question/math-academy-id "q-165787" :question/problem "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of $\\,{R}^{2}.$ If $T$ is a linear transformation such that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=2j,$ then what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $T?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $\\,i\\,$ and  $\\,j.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/34c82826da26290fc890671734c354fbfd9c253f1cfaca38de1ea615434e6766.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=2j,$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $T:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/56a5f95d469cb63e20936a6c07971d9e80611d966282cb37d7ddf17fdc761bab.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch with a scale factor $2$ in the $y$-direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69bac3fa-d409-5c16-87b2-383423e8d754" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square Under a Shear or Stretch Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bfe53a0-d63f-5285-8d89-c2b5b561fa8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/46e48817af724645b2dde55f6aa55a48c44b5160de6aebec0b1da5cc7ef1e79c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342581} {:answer/id #uuid "28813f25-d10a-5c2b-901f-49d755aceed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/bfdc9898972e72a5d15c8313eff3403e2ea712e2bffe52b3fd1d9fb6156b7bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342582} {:answer/id #uuid "2519178e-a642-58f2-abe0-1d9d7088099f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/364633d020e330780fab5ea588cca231ecd0e3d3c8e3e5516e4d6d0d990299c0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342583} {:answer/id #uuid "d8af1d75-698f-5410-9382-cbc69112b081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/adaef18865941a58a89a221c40f8e44aa6b95def80d12a08897c7be34ae7a9aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342584} {:answer/id #uuid "a54a940b-885e-5cb5-9997-21b1f954a859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/dae63d7007d115438139be51862d878deadd1ed300b3b870935c2cbe44edaef3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28813f25-d10a-5c2b-901f-49d755aceed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/bfdc9898972e72a5d15c8313eff3403e2ea712e2bffe52b3fd1d9fb6156b7bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342582} :answer-field/id #uuid "0066a0a2-7300-54c2-9f1d-eee2499d2904" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80a9d60b-2d00-5629-b0f1-438f9ef2a843" :question/math-academy-id "q-165980" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/611f958a7bd7475e37f743ef86affde7754195fe2c9ad414e628c17a09d47e38.png)\n\nLet $T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $T$ represents a horizontal shear of shear factor $1.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $T$ is\n\n  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/cfe398e1ab7e5b4b487671772bf4b6127e8e141b25491f96c5cc32e77869dec0.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the parallelogram shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3105e8eb-010f-542b-9e40-12fda6ea00e7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Matrix of a Stretch Transformation Given a Vector and its Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7baaa823-4317-5893-8760-58522db305c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7baaa823-4317-5893-8760-58522db305c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342588} :answer-field/id #uuid "933df4d0-3509-5e79-bee9-96659673b0f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342587} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51f3b604-b001-538c-8b3c-6f6cd8a80111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51f3b604-b001-538c-8b3c-6f6cd8a80111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342590} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac01ecf7-6a98-5a9f-9280-2eb5a9d411d0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342589} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64df85fa-2ea4-575f-99f4-b40e6ba89cd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64df85fa-2ea4-575f-99f4-b40e6ba89cd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342592} :answer-field/id #uuid "d633505d-dab1-550f-9042-2483c3b82ab9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4879405b-2d19-5d7e-99f6-9f2899744e7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-317606" :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(u)=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u)$ are the same, while\n\n- the $x$-component of $T(u)$ is $3$ times the $x$-component of $u.$\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $3$ in the $x$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3105e8eb-010f-542b-9e40-12fda6ea00e7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Matrix of a Stretch Transformation Given a Vector and its Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bc3c941-eb66-5555-8d60-181fe24c7468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bc3c941-eb66-5555-8d60-181fe24c7468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342595} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8e1473c-1de5-54a0-9237-35db5a9f49d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342594} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d68253fd-ff6e-5953-ba8e-bf0bdc15728a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d68253fd-ff6e-5953-ba8e-bf0bdc15728a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342597} :answer-field/id #uuid "856855d4-bc04-506c-9ee3-afedc09171f8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342596} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "391aa0a5-532c-55d0-ad07-9472cf2e0701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "391aa0a5-532c-55d0-ad07-9472cf2e0701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342599} :answer-field/id #uuid "517a3563-445f-5c1d-8247-ba695b632bea" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "269e92e7-dd9e-5a56-9cb9-162b4eeb2056" :question/math-academy-id "q-317609" :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(u)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u)$ are the same, while\n\n- the $x$-component of $T(u)$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the $x$-component of $u.$\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3105e8eb-010f-542b-9e40-12fda6ea00e7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Matrix of a Stretch Transformation Given a Vector and its Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7543c6eb-e15d-5105-8824-c47648ce8987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342602} {:answer/id #uuid "8bb58a72-7e75-5153-af35-f855c7ebcc06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bb58a72-7e75-5153-af35-f855c7ebcc06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342603} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3bf16ba-e936-5c3b-8838-41bfcee22fff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342601} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d539fa5-0dce-5c21-8a46-446d8a611aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d539fa5-0dce-5c21-8a46-446d8a611aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342605} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f866802-2694-55e2-be5b-7133e65d14a0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342604} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e39b567-84e7-5721-9114-270e18bed224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e39b567-84e7-5721-9114-270e18bed224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342607} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf669ff6-d42c-5423-97ff-2bb59403dff5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342606} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82fcfe0d-7fe1-5c12-8d20-36602f181190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82fcfe0d-7fe1-5c12-8d20-36602f181190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342609} :answer-field/id #uuid "d39177ba-dec4-5a66-82da-bbba3ba42378" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "964ace1f-4db9-5484-a2c3-7ffb0c8d5586" :question/math-academy-id "q-317612" :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(u)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-components of $u$ and $T(u)$ are the same, while\n\n- the $y$-component of $T(u)$ is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the $y$-component of $u.$\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $\\sqrt{3}$ in the $y$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be42e4b3-4b15-589c-a7e1-23cc07083aff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Given the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f07abba3-0be4-5595-a64a-a4b384de9c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{3}$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342612} {:answer/id #uuid "b2eb2078-c629-5cba-8e94-17ea2035657b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342613} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6a25a1-4124-5b2d-82f0-2534897a7429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $3$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342614} {:answer/id #uuid "aebf5235-9622-55b5-9382-5b94faedb45d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342615} {:answer/id #uuid "5e74e378-c1a3-56b6-8eee-666e5d0cc418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $3$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f6a25a1-4124-5b2d-82f0-2534897a7429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $3$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342614} :answer-field/id #uuid "19494819-2f98-5121-9151-eab154e7d3ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8007798-26fd-5f48-9d8f-a07b9c92e47f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38020" :question/problem "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of $\\,{R}^{2}.$ If $T$ is a linear transformation such that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=3j,$ then what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $T?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $\\,i\\,$ and  $\\,j.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/f2438da7f6f0a2ab7e6653cad7776ef7d01faba55d22a480a98c152e81f0d78a.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=3j,$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $T:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/8674e7345c903bf193e09cc9ae1702383cad10ba06c5dc3b6d2713ea30eb843e.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch with a scale factor $3$ in the $y$-direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be42e4b3-4b15-589c-a7e1-23cc07083aff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Given the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c683b49-f6bb-5acb-9d17-84206cfc5a1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $1.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103920} {:answer/id #uuid "9e2106a7-b49c-543e-a322-1a9fa6bf6e2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $2.5$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103921} {:answer/id #uuid "2639659d-85d9-5faa-be2f-dfbf3d48ab9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $1.5$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103922} {:answer/id #uuid "3184ea3b-c017-511e-b474-90e157e3cd20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $1.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103923} {:answer/id #uuid "68e3cb33-d35c-56e4-b8a7-21f193ac1a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $1.5$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3184ea3b-c017-511e-b474-90e157e3cd20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $1.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103923} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f4476f2-f0bb-5620-8663-b9f0e1397b7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d5441e20-477a-5032-8e85-180d271e5aa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-38052" :question/problem "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of ${R}^{2}.$ If $T$ is a linear transformation such that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=1.5i+j,$ then what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $T?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $i$ and $j.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/db15a65fd944c24ea2545baea08e6a0789b749295f623d8d4fdc2f07a37d4b73.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=1.5i+j,$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $T:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/f5ebbff856529ad8cd3da41bf2f7d373294af3e0153c5e8da7da4c7b069b8b25.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ represents a horizontal shear with a shear factor of $1.5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69bac3fa-d409-5c16-87b2-383423e8d754" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square Under a Shear or Stretch Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "869570ef-da66-5f87-b516-a2bd3252566c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/q-38066-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103926} {:answer/id #uuid "07b11424-f45d-566a-9c13-cc4b0f6b6d10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/q-38066-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103927} {:answer/id #uuid "3aacca3b-51f5-5d23-861c-c8ed2328d698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/q-38066-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103928} {:answer/id #uuid "41cde7cf-b668-59bf-856d-50476890a71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/q-38066-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103929} {:answer/id #uuid "666ed508-40b1-50c8-b2e8-869db22f2f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/q-38066-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "666ed508-40b1-50c8-b2e8-869db22f2f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/q-38066-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103930} :answer-field/id #uuid "e78ff40b-f464-5fc3-8a4b-48ab231fbdd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e7b74ac9-0349-5e05-ad52-99e0f4d0d1b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-38066" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/611f958a7bd7475e37f743ef86affde7754195fe2c9ad414e628c17a09d47e38.png)\n\nLet $T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $T$ represents a horizontal shear of shear factor $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $T$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/85ff90a404a22310659fadd3a00c58490c9745d443b180d6b509b40a5497376b.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the parallelogram shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69bac3fa-d409-5c16-87b2-383423e8d754" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square Under a Shear or Stretch Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "622cff06-9867-523c-899a-28136f6af4c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/82c6d9267a249e4beb17fa30753e0c4f11cecc88a3c26289d25b02eb7ce6b850.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342619} {:answer/id #uuid "3618eb9b-e6bc-553f-b2ad-42f92c4aef1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/fcb92d172a66567277e8619839a9d3bb4288bbe225850692eeed7267ee4d1292.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342620} {:answer/id #uuid "8c164c6f-c58c-5040-b4e0-5810c7dd63c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/52bb468dfeaae8b4c47cb08a7bb08e671e898467e4130e2e243936b599f51704.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342621} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d2b8cc-d5a2-543c-8070-3baca1ee1aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/59a9db0d3848f29b1e3aafe4f485a61953e7e21822bc3e5111d7aac8bc6b8993.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342622} {:answer/id #uuid "bc42e6bd-7b8d-5722-87a6-e48008a5eefa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/5b8e80ca6209eeb01ddad113b796d65fc7a60e9036594759780489321091bc42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc42e6bd-7b8d-5722-87a6-e48008a5eefa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/5b8e80ca6209eeb01ddad113b796d65fc7a60e9036594759780489321091bc42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342623} :answer-field/id #uuid "c18b1bbe-fc63-5132-86c3-6bb573ba1623" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e58e56de-8ca3-56ed-a744-47b3e23fdfdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-38070" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/611f958a7bd7475e37f743ef86affde7754195fe2c9ad414e628c17a09d47e38.png)\n\nLet $T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) under the linear transformation $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $T$ represents a horizontal shear of shear factor $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $T$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/4b6cfc485ffa74f141e3dc4b8fad61b48103808a4f4c29b2dc5180f634902c4f.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the parallelogram shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3105e8eb-010f-542b-9e40-12fda6ea00e7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Matrix of a Stretch Transformation Given a Vector and its Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8397036e-ce84-5921-a652-a92254eec68d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105018} {:answer/id #uuid "99a49945-72b3-51fc-8a64-f4ed779e1700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105019} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e58735-679e-5f23-8d0f-39db3f609c53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105020} {:answer/id #uuid "df50c58e-c2f7-58e0-adba-dbecf8c52bc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105021} {:answer/id #uuid "087212dd-4e03-52a6-84d6-0287b2159b53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df50c58e-c2f7-58e0-adba-dbecf8c52bc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105021} :answer-field/id #uuid "17b9b93b-24ff-5b97-bdd1-5eb57a2a88b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9beb837-2a5f-5628-9a45-88a8085c9d8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80725" :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $v$ and $T(v),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-components of $v$ and $T(v)$ are the same, while\n\n- the $y$-component of $T(v)$ is $\\frac{1}{3}$ times the $y$-component of $v.$\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{3}$ in the $y$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3105e8eb-010f-542b-9e40-12fda6ea00e7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Matrix of a Stretch Transformation Given a Vector and its Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89f93f56-ac75-57b5-95c1-f90f361f5930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105024} {:answer/id #uuid "b33af94e-420f-5cfd-b0af-f118b8697055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105025} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6bae65-78c2-5856-9f0b-163e1c048ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105026} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0d689b-1c5e-51c2-88cc-ac6b5d900a1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105027} {:answer/id #uuid "2aff7b31-5277-574c-afb7-f649464edb91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf6bae65-78c2-5856-9f0b-163e1c048ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105026} :answer-field/id #uuid "637d172d-46f4-5b21-8f12-e315dda47530" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85f5eecb-79b7-5107-8347-e7d15fcb457b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80726" :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $v$ and $T(v),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-component of $T(v)$ is $2$ times the $x$-component of $v,$ while\n\n- the $y$-components of $v$ and $T(v)$ are the same.\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $2$ in the $x$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c36ce626-1562-51f8-b3fd-d7dc7ce8659c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Represented by a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13e77a2b-7fa1-5405-b71e-229e8934cf46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342626} {:answer/id #uuid "26012cd5-bde5-5ce4-b6c3-1807b394fa42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342627} {:answer/id #uuid "e96aef8d-9b5f-58a7-81ab-4ea099683517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $2$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342628} {:answer/id #uuid "3d37ad42-1354-55cc-8671-d526cd110f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $2$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342629} {:answer/id #uuid "09b32c15-ae7f-5aa7-94eb-68362184f1ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d37ad42-1354-55cc-8671-d526cd110f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $2$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342629} :answer-field/id #uuid "caa9150a-471c-58de-8d0f-e08773258e4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29e3b0ce-5fa2-5790-b9cd-94a978876673" :question/math-academy-id "q-8089" :question/problem "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the following matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of  $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=2i \\\\ T(j) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a stretch of scale factor $2$ in the $x$-direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c36ce626-1562-51f8-b3fd-d7dc7ce8659c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Represented by a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbe0a344-dfa2-57cf-b566-0b1c21ed0d44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342633} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7cb190-95e5-5253-a016-8e60d9688bf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $5$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342634} {:answer/id #uuid "16ad5af6-908e-50bc-aa3f-d01c946add6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342635} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0009f1-ffe4-542a-a3c3-93a77b1267eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342636} {:answer/id #uuid "9242007d-e92a-5b30-aa39-bee3733ef2f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $5$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a7cb190-95e5-5253-a016-8e60d9688bf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $5$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342634} :answer-field/id #uuid "b139f62a-d382-5833-a73d-234211e600b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "372f4cef-22c3-5d56-a2ed-97f61a4ca270" :question/math-academy-id "q-8090" :question/problem "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the following matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of  $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ T(j) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=5j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a stretch of scale factor $5$ in the $y$-direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c36ce626-1562-51f8-b3fd-d7dc7ce8659c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Represented by a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97b497ef-4e78-56c7-b466-bab4193e14bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105030} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c9f3eb-76ff-56f4-bc2d-be4b2d00dfaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $12$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105031} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3e86a3-7190-5acf-90b4-604d0334e7a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $12$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105032} {:answer/id #uuid "5f264d87-719c-5ea4-8a2f-fbfad9d2df8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{12}$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105033} {:answer/id #uuid "63adcb45-2209-598c-8bef-ddf79754b6c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97b497ef-4e78-56c7-b466-bab4193e14bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105030} :answer-field/id #uuid "f327005f-2bad-5ed7-b6ad-08f19f4c3000" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c971f14c-4455-5076-82e3-a0ff1ee68da4" :question/math-academy-id "q-8092" :question/problem "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of  $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]=i+12j \\\\ T(j) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a *vertical* shear with a shear factor of $12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be42e4b3-4b15-589c-a7e1-23cc07083aff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Given the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ab2ea59-2da1-5ede-863e-a8aa6bced4bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342640} {:answer/id #uuid "f4813398-a4f3-5854-bba8-999c9bec8467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342641} {:answer/id #uuid "bf1b5815-3471-511e-8445-18f82cfc5bfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $2$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342642} {:answer/id #uuid "d12f6dd2-369d-576d-9769-c1f7748c0108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342643} {:answer/id #uuid "f9474545-b5ac-5ffc-ae9d-3f7b8fa71ac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d12f6dd2-369d-576d-9769-c1f7748c0108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342643} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a3fee8e-9ef3-5a0a-8295-3545a26e79a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5883242f-b44f-5ff7-9fa7-30bcd180241a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81324" :question/problem "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of ${R}^{2}.$ If $T$ is a linear transformation such that $T(i)=\\frac{1}{2}i$ and $T(j)=j,$ then what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $T?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $i$ and  $j.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/34c82826da26290fc890671734c354fbfd9c253f1cfaca38de1ea615434e6766.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $T(i)=\\frac{1}{2}i$ and $T(j)=j,$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $T:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/02470083dea4808af1854e9ed88ab496828a429c593af780209d8a6d4b15a6b1.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch with a scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be42e4b3-4b15-589c-a7e1-23cc07083aff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Given the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46fe7a23-5ecb-5b60-a944-104db522fbe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $-1$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342647} {:answer/id #uuid "a393c261-7ca6-5adb-8e44-ed491cd36371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342648} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9be6f7-cf02-5730-b1a7-d8eef71b856c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342649} {:answer/id #uuid "5a553c26-4c66-534d-a5f0-c0c5bf3d742e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $-1$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342650} {:answer/id #uuid "ffaa0540-8e1b-5148-8a85-3a6e7a5a04fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d9be6f7-cf02-5730-b1a7-d8eef71b856c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342649} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba94d43d-1c4f-5197-9748-bcebe4547256" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "32a103ba-7e9c-5ed8-b8b8-915a57355296" :question/math-academy-id "q-81327" :question/problem "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of ${R}^{2}.$ If $T$ is a linear transformation such that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=-i+j,$ then what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $T?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $i$ and $j.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/db15a65fd944c24ea2545baea08e6a0789b749295f623d8d4fdc2f07a37d4b73.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=-i+j,$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $T:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/cee26b17d477344e138c3d6d9228f23d67c4c78ce447b1c2c3a887b79125ee7a.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ represents a horizontal shear with a shear factor of $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69bac3fa-d409-5c16-87b2-383423e8d754" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square Under a Shear or Stretch Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80890a78-ad4a-5e73-8a38-439e488a3b04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/a64061e8714d38748ef4907cbdedf873355c64814ef3966d872494e84bcacfbd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342654} {:answer/id #uuid "9e94137a-820e-52a5-a285-ebf6c9502040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/52bb468dfeaae8b4c47cb08a7bb08e671e898467e4130e2e243936b599f51704.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342655} {:answer/id #uuid "493cbc33-bb06-5a31-91e0-1d039b65d51a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/6a5da4ecc4dc4cb550649d229f0c7c9d64bd713f901084a937785ea93df86eb1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342656} {:answer/id #uuid "0c5bae6e-d775-5d97-a0f5-e89e4109e88f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/9d140b11016d68a8372623e997abdc588633ee02b56a8edee61c8cb269fd4c20.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342657} {:answer/id #uuid "935a1e3e-58e7-5254-abdc-9b13208612f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/1d25632f24abfc3176f515ba41a763e166d8c9c3101d07732a1efb183a2e9d75.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "493cbc33-bb06-5a31-91e0-1d039b65d51a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/6a5da4ecc4dc4cb550649d229f0c7c9d64bd713f901084a937785ea93df86eb1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342656} :answer-field/id #uuid "c55f7bfd-2279-5694-99a0-c0dd25b2c002" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7cd28c9-29e0-5184-9921-9c2ab31af8ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-81413" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/611f958a7bd7475e37f743ef86affde7754195fe2c9ad414e628c17a09d47e38.png)\n\nLet $T=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $T$ represents a stretch in the $x$-direction of scale factor $\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $T$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/72c88d1df90c3462d7f1f186c06b9a7acf30c071f5ba8bbf722ee125fee0e616.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the rectangle shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69bac3fa-d409-5c16-87b2-383423e8d754" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square Under a Shear or Stretch Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2935dd-0d6d-55a9-ab97-d4417cc3aa92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/a64061e8714d38748ef4907cbdedf873355c64814ef3966d872494e84bcacfbd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342661} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6aee0e-e89f-5916-8b6d-0083b2917210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/52bb468dfeaae8b4c47cb08a7bb08e671e898467e4130e2e243936b599f51704.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342662} {:answer/id #uuid "641609eb-21cf-5cf5-8ae1-0bd306e16940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/6a5da4ecc4dc4cb550649d229f0c7c9d64bd713f901084a937785ea93df86eb1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342663} {:answer/id #uuid "bef2fcf9-4c3f-5285-a7db-f6b78a01eabb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/9d140b11016d68a8372623e997abdc588633ee02b56a8edee61c8cb269fd4c20.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342664} {:answer/id #uuid "278f01a1-0938-5e51-be58-d007e63747ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/1d25632f24abfc3176f515ba41a763e166d8c9c3101d07732a1efb183a2e9d75.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bef2fcf9-4c3f-5285-a7db-f6b78a01eabb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/9d140b11016d68a8372623e997abdc588633ee02b56a8edee61c8cb269fd4c20.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342664} :answer-field/id #uuid "18ac4f57-381e-5664-ab4b-64747125ab5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5dc26e64-ac7b-5866-bdbe-d8e8e4000f11" :question/math-academy-id "q-81417" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/611f958a7bd7475e37f743ef86affde7754195fe2c9ad414e628c17a09d47e38.png)\n\nLet $T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $T$ represents a stretch in the $y$-direction of scale factor $\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $T$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/7d321ec7760226fbdf2c1d539f5d9da92fa27abf84a2890702273da41df75848.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the rectangle shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
